INT. SLEEPER TRAIN - DINING CAR - NIGHT

White tablecloths. Swaying chandeliers. Rain drums the windows. Five passengers sit at a long table set for dinner.

DETECTIVE HORTENSE VANDERBILT-OKAFOR (50s, tweed) cuts her steak. She watches her left. He pushes his food around, shaking. She watches her right. They sip wine, calm, bored.

MR. LEOPOLD PRUITT (60s, banker) coughs into a napkin. He glances at the clock. MRS. GRETA LINDQVIST (40s, black gloves) dabs her mouth. She studies the door.

CAPTAIN DESMOND TREVELYAN (40s, uniform) sits across from them, jaw tight. He taps the table. His glass trembles.

YOUNG MISS ADELAIDE FOX (20s, curls) looks at her plate. She has not eaten.

The train enters a tunnel. The lights flicker. A scream, short and cut off. The lights return.

Pruitt slumps in his chair, a steak knife in his chest. She leaps up. He gasps. They freeze. He knocks over his water. She slaps the table.

                    HORTENSE
          Nobody move.

She crosses to the body and lifts his wrist. She shakes her head. She looks at each face in turn, and they stare back.

                    TREVELYAN
          Detective, I assure you--

                    HORTENSE
          Captain, sit down.

He sits. She studies the knife, then the table settings.

                    GRETA
          (barely a whisper)
          That was my knife.

All heads turn to her. She holds up her hands, palms open, gloves still on.

                    GRETA (CONT'D)
          I mean, it was set at my place. Someone switched it.

                    ADELAIDE
          Detective, the train is locked.

                    HORTENSE
          Yes, Miss Fox. It is.

She picks up the cold dinner roll from her plate, tears it in half, and sets it down.

                    HORTENSE (CONT'D)
          Then one of you is lying.
